# Source for q/spi-doppler2.js: second Doppler + Hemodynamics batch (controls, waveforms, calculations, artifacts, vascular).
D="Doppler"; H="Hemodynamics"
Q=[
# --- Doppler equation calculations ---
(D,"Transmitted frequency 5 MHz, blood velocity 0.77 m/s, angle 0°, speed 1540 m/s. What is the Doppler shift?","5,000 Hz",["2,500 Hz","500 Hz","50,000 Hz"],"Δf = 2 × v × f₀ × cos θ ÷ c = 2 × 0.77 × 5,000,000 ÷ 1540 = 5,000 Hz.","C"),
(D,"The same blood flow (5 MHz, 0.77 m/s) is insonated at 60°. What is the Doppler shift now?","2,500 Hz",["5,000 Hz","0 Hz","4,330 Hz"],"cos 60° = 0.5, so the shift halves to 2,500 Hz.","C"),
(D,"Changing the angle from 0° to 90° makes the Doppler shift:","Fall to zero",["Double","Stay the same","Become maximal"],"cos 90° = 0.","F"),
(D,"If transmitted frequency is halved and velocity doubles, the Doppler shift:","Stays the same",["Doubles","Halves","Quadruples"],"Shift is proportional to both, so the two changes cancel.","C"),
(D,"In vascular Doppler, angle correction is usually kept at or below:","60°",["90°","5°","80°"],"At larger angles small errors in the angle cursor cause big velocity errors.","F"),
(D,"Why do angle errors matter more at 70° than at 20°?","The cosine changes much faster at large angles, so a small angle error causes a large velocity error",["Cosine is constant at large angles","Doppler shift is maximal at 70°","The PRF changes with angle"],"Near 90° the cosine falls steeply; near 0° it barely changes.","C"),
(D,"Angle correction in spectral Doppler tells the machine:","The angle between the beam and the flow direction so it can calculate true velocity",["The depth of the gate","The transmitted frequency","The color priority"],"Align the angle cursor parallel to the vessel wall or flow jet.","F"),
(D,"The Doppler frequency used in a duplex transducer is often lower than the imaging frequency because:","Lower frequency penetrates better and is less prone to aliasing",["Lower frequency improves axial resolution","It raises the Doppler shift","It increases the frame rate"],"Smaller Doppler shifts are less likely to exceed the Nyquist limit.","F"),
(D,"Doppler shift is inversely related to:","Propagation speed in the medium",["Transmitted frequency","Reflector velocity","Cosine of the angle"],"Since speed is fixed in soft tissue, this term rarely changes in practice.","F"),
# --- Controls ---
(D,"Spectral Doppler gain adjusts:","The brightness of the spectral display",["The transmitted power","The PRF","The Doppler angle"],"Too much gain causes noise and mirror images; too little loses weak signals.","F"),
(D,"To record the true peak velocity in a vessel, the sample volume should be:","Small and placed in the center of the flow, or at the narrowest point of a stenosis",["Large and covering the whole vessel","Placed against the vessel wall","Placed outside the vessel"],"A small gate reduces spectral broadening from slower flow near the walls.","F"),
(D,"Steering the color box with a linear array is used to:","Improve the Doppler angle to a flat vessel",["Raise the frame rate","Reduce aliasing by raising frequency","Remove the need for angle correction"],"A vessel parallel to the skin is perpendicular to an unsteered beam, giving little or no Doppler shift.","F"),
(D,"The 'heel-toe' maneuver with a linear transducer is used to:","Change the angle between the beam and a superficial vessel",["Increase output power","Reduce gain","Change the PRF"],"Pressing one end of the probe tilts the beam relative to the vessel.","F"),
(D,"The invert control on a color or spectral display:","Flips the display so flow toward appears below the baseline or colors swap",["Changes the real flow direction","Raises the Nyquist limit","Corrects aliasing"],"Always check the color bar before deciding flow direction.","F"),
(D,"The color bar beside the image shows:","The colors for each direction and the Nyquist limits in each direction",["Gain settings","Frame rate","Depth"],"The top of the bar is usually flow toward the transducer.","F"),
(D,"Raising the color velocity scale will:","Reduce aliasing but lower sensitivity to slow flow",["Increase aliasing","Increase sensitivity to slow flow","Lower the frame rate only"],"Set the scale to fit the expected velocities.","F"),
(D,"Update (freeze-and-sample) mode in duplex Doppler:","Pauses the 2D image so the spectral Doppler can use more time and a higher PRF",["Runs 2D and Doppler simultaneously at full rate","Turns off Doppler","Raises output power"],"Simultaneous duplex shares time between modes, lowering PRF and frame rate.","C"),
(D,"Phase quadrature detection in a Doppler receiver is used to:","Determine the direction of flow",["Measure flow speed only","Reduce aliasing","Improve lateral resolution"],"It compares two signals 90° apart to tell toward from away.","C"),
(D,"Range gating in pulsed Doppler means:","Listening for echoes only from a chosen depth",["Using two crystals","Removing low-frequency signals","Averaging velocities over the beam"],"It gives PW its range resolution.","F"),
(D,"A larger color box (taller and wider) will:","Lower the frame rate",["Raise the frame rate","Raise the Nyquist limit","Improve spatial resolution"],"Deeper boxes also lower the PRF and Nyquist limit.","F"),
(D,"Color Doppler persistence (averaging) helps show:","Slow or weak flow more consistently, but blurs rapid changes",["Peak velocity","Aliasing","Exact timing of jets"],"Lower persistence is better for fast, brief jets in the heart.","C"),
(D,"Color line density affects:","How finely color is sampled across the box, trading detail for frame rate",["The transmitted frequency","Doppler angle","Wall filter"],"More color lines give a smoother color map but fewer frames.","C"),
(D,"Increasing the wall filter in color Doppler removes:","Low-velocity signals, including tissue motion and slow flow",["High-velocity jets","Aliasing","Turbulence"],"Use a higher filter to suppress flash, a lower one to show slow flow.","F"),
(D,"Spectral Doppler 'smoothing' or 'compression' controls change:","How the spectral display looks, not the underlying measured velocities",["The Doppler angle","The true peak velocity","The PRF"],"Display processing can make the envelope easier to trace.","C"),
# --- Spectral waveform interpretation ---
(D,"A monophasic, low-resistance waveform with continuous diastolic flow is normal in the:","Internal carotid artery",["Common femoral artery at rest","Brachial artery at rest","External carotid artery"],"Organs with constant demand (brain, kidney, liver) have low-resistance beds.","F"),
(D,"A high-resistance waveform with sharp upstroke, brief reversal and little diastolic flow is normal in:","Resting extremity arteries",["The internal carotid artery","The renal artery","The hepatic artery"],"Vasoconstricted muscle beds create the triphasic pattern.","F"),
(D,"After exercise, a resting limb artery waveform becomes:","Low resistance with more diastolic flow",["More triphasic","Absent","Reversed"],"Muscle vasodilation lowers resistance. Reactive hyperemia does the same.","F"),
(D,"A blunted, monophasic waveform in the common femoral artery suggests:","A significant stenosis or occlusion upstream (proximal)",["Normal resting flow","A distal stenosis only","Venous obstruction"],"Flow beyond an obstruction is damped (tardus parvus).","F"),
(D,"A 'thump' (high-resistance, low-velocity) waveform proximal to a stenosis suggests:","A severe obstruction downstream",["A normal vessel","An upstream stenosis","Vasodilation"],"Blood bounces off the blockage ahead, raising resistance in the segment before it.","C"),
(D,"Within a stenosis, the Doppler spectrum shows:","High peak velocity",["Low peak velocity","No flow","Reversed flow only"],"Velocity rises to keep flow constant through the narrower area (continuity).","F"),
(D,"Just downstream of a stenosis, the Doppler spectrum shows:","Turbulence with spectral broadening and possible flow reversal",["A clean spectral window","No flow","Normal triphasic flow"],"Post-stenotic turbulence is one of the best signs of a significant narrowing.","F"),
(D,"Normal lower-extremity venous Doppler is:","Spontaneous, phasic with respiration, and augments with distal compression",["Pulsatile like an artery","Continuous with no respiratory change","Absent at rest"],"Loss of phasicity suggests obstruction between the probe and the heart.","F"),
(D,"Pulsatile flow in the femoral veins suggests:","Raised right heart pressure, such as from heart failure or severe TR",["Normal finding in all adults","Deep vein thrombosis","Arterial stenosis"],"Right heart pressures are transmitted back to the leg veins.","F"),
(D,"Continuous, non-phasic venous flow in the common femoral vein suggests:","Obstruction proximal to the probe, such as iliac vein thrombosis",["Normal flow","Right heart failure","Arterial disease"],"The obstruction damps respiratory changes.","F"),
(D,"Venous reflux is defined by:","Reversed flow lasting longer than a set time after a maneuver (about 0.5 s in superficial veins, 1 s in deep veins)",["Any forward flow","Flow with respiration","Arterial flow in a vein"],"Valsalva or distal compression with release is used to test valves.","C"),
(D,"In the hepatic veins, a normal Doppler pattern is:","Phasic and pulsatile, with S and D waves toward the heart",["Continuous and flat","Systolic reversal","Absent"],"Systolic reversal suggests severe TR.","F"),
(D,"The acceleration time on a spectral waveform is:","The time from the start of systole to peak velocity",["The time from peak to end-diastole","The pulse repetition period","The time between heartbeats"],"A prolonged acceleration time downstream suggests proximal obstruction.","F"),
(D,"Peak systolic velocity is measured at:","The top edge of the spectral envelope at its highest point",["The baseline","The center of the brightest band","The end of diastole"],"Mean velocity is the time-averaged middle; modal velocity is the brightest band.","F"),
(D,"The modal velocity on a spectral display is:","The velocity of the greatest number of red blood cells (the brightest line)",["The highest velocity","The lowest velocity","The average of peak and minimum"],"It is shown as the brightest band in the spectrum.","C"),
(D,"Time-averaged mean velocity (TAMV) is used to calculate:","Volume flow, together with vessel area",["Peak gradient","Resistive index","Nyquist limit"],"Flow = TAMV × area. In echo, VTI × area gives stroke volume.","C"),
# --- Color Doppler interpretation ---
(D,"The brightest shades on a color map represent:","Higher mean velocities in that direction",["Lower velocities","Turbulence only","Tissue motion"],"Darker shades near the baseline represent slow flow.","F"),
(D,"A 'mosaic' color pattern in a jet represents:","Turbulence and aliasing",["Laminar flow","No flow","Tissue motion"],"It is typical of stenotic and regurgitant jets.","F"),
(D,"A vessel that looks red at one end and blue at the other, with a black line at the point where it curves, is:","Normal flow changing direction relative to the beam",["Aliasing","A stenosis","An occlusion"],"Where flow is perpendicular to the beam, no Doppler shift is detected.","F"),
(D,"Color fills only part of a vessel's lumen. The most likely causes include:","Thrombus or plaque, or color settings that are not sensitive enough",["Perfect settings","Aliasing only","High color gain"],"Optimize scale, gain and angle before calling a filling defect.","F"),
(D,"Color is absent in a vessel known to have flow. Which setting is most likely wrong?","Velocity scale too high or wall filter too high",["Color gain too high","Scale too low","Packet size too large"],"Also check the angle: a 90° angle gives no color.","F"),
(D,"Twinkle artifact in color Doppler is seen:","Behind rough, strongly reflective surfaces such as calcifications or stones",["Inside normal blood vessels","Only in veins","In cysts"],"It appears as rapidly changing color and can help identify small stones.","C"),
(D,"Color Doppler blooming occurs when:","Color gain is too high, so color spreads beyond the vessel walls",["Gain is too low","Wall filter is too high","Scale is too high"],"It can hide small plaques or make vessels look wider.","F"),
(D,"A color 'flash' artifact over the heart or near pulsating vessels is best reduced by:","Raising the wall filter or reducing color gain",["Lowering the wall filter","Raising color gain","Lowering the scale"],"Flash comes from tissue motion. Power Doppler is especially prone to it.","F"),
(D,"Color aliasing at the narrowest point of a stenosis is useful because it:","Marks where the highest velocities are, guiding the spectral sample volume",["Means the machine is broken","Shows the stenosis is mild","Removes the need for spectral Doppler"],"Place the PW or CW Doppler at the aliasing point to measure peak velocity.","F"),
(D,"Ghosting (color in tissue next to a vessel) can be caused by:","Motion of strong reflectors such as valves or vessel walls",["A clean spectral window","Low color gain","A narrow color box"],"Raise the wall filter or adjust gain.","C"),
(D,"Power Doppler is especially useful for:","Detecting slow or low-volume flow, such as in small vessels or near-occlusions",["Measuring peak velocity","Showing flow direction","Diagnosing aliasing"],"It is more sensitive but gives no direction or velocity information.","F"),
(D,"Power Doppler is more prone than color Doppler to:","Flash artifact from tissue motion",["Aliasing","Range ambiguity","Mirror image of the spectrum"],"Slow frame rates and sensitivity to any motion make flash common.","F"),
# --- Spectral artifacts and pitfalls ---
(D,"Spectral broadening that is caused by too much gain rather than turbulence can be identified by:","Lowering the gain and seeing the window clear",["Raising the wall filter","Increasing the PRF","Using CW Doppler"],"True turbulence persists when gain is reduced.","F"),
(D,"Using a large sample volume in a small vessel causes:","Spectral broadening from sampling slow flow near the walls",["A cleaner window","Aliasing only","Mirror image only"],"Size the gate to the center of the lumen when measuring velocities.","F"),
(D,"A Doppler signal appearing equally above and below the baseline with the beam at 90° to the vessel is due to:","Beam spread: parts of the beam see flow toward and away (spectral mirror)",["Aliasing","Turbulence","Range ambiguity"],"Improve the angle and reduce gain.","C"),
(D,"Spectral Doppler aliasing can be distinguished from bidirectional flow because aliased signals:","Wrap from the top of the scale to the bottom, continuing the same waveform",["Show two separate waveforms","Always show a clean window","Appear only in diastole"],"Raise the scale, shift the baseline or switch to CW to confirm.","F"),
(D,"Why does CW Doppler often show a 'filled-in' spectrum?","It records all velocities along the whole beam overlap",["It aliases","It uses too much gain","It has a small gate"],"Spectral broadening is expected in CW and doesn't mean turbulence.","F"),
(D,"When estimating velocity with PW Doppler at a 0° angle, which source of error remains?","Beam not truly parallel with the flow jet",["Cosine error at 60°","Aliasing never occurs","Gate depth"],"In echo, the jet direction is not always known, so velocities can still be underestimated.","C"),
# --- Hemodynamics ---
(H,"Volume flow (Q) equals:","Pressure difference ÷ resistance",["Pressure × resistance","Resistance ÷ pressure","Velocity ÷ area"],"Like Ohm's law: flow rises with the pressure gradient and falls with resistance.","F"),
(H,"Poiseuille's law shows that flow rate is directly proportional to:","Pressure difference and radius to the fourth power",["Viscosity and length","Length only","Viscosity only"],"Q = ΔP × π × r⁴ ÷ (8 × η × L).","F"),
(H,"If vessel radius doubles, with pressure unchanged, flow rate:","Increases 16 times",["Doubles","Quadruples","Halves"],"2⁴ = 16.","C"),
(H,"Doubling vessel length, with everything else constant, makes resistance:","Double",["Halve","Increase 16 times","Stay the same"],"Resistance is directly proportional to length.","F"),
(H,"Resistances in series combine by:","Adding them together",["Adding their reciprocals","Multiplying them","Taking the largest one only"],"Total resistance of vessels in series is always greater than any single one.","C"),
(H,"Resistances in parallel (such as capillary beds) combine so that total resistance is:","Less than any single resistance",["Greater than any single resistance","Equal to the largest one","Equal to their sum"],"1 ÷ R_total = 1 ÷ R₁ + 1 ÷ R₂ + ... This is why parallel collateral vessels lower resistance.","C"),
(H,"Velocity of blood is fastest where:","Total cross-sectional area is smallest, such as the aorta",["Total area is largest, such as capillaries","Viscosity is highest","Pressure is highest"],"Capillaries are tiny but there are so many that their total area is huge, so flow is slow.","C"),
(H,"Kinetic energy of flowing blood depends on:","Density and velocity squared",["Pressure only","Height only","Viscosity"],"KE = ½ × ρ × v². In a stenosis, kinetic energy rises as pressure falls (Bernoulli).","F"),
(H,"The main form of energy that keeps blood moving is:","Pressure (potential) energy created by the heart",["Kinetic energy only","Gravitational energy only","Heat"],"Pressure energy is converted to kinetic energy and lost to friction along the way.","F"),
(H,"Compliance (capacitance) of a vessel describes:","How easily it stretches to hold more blood",["Its resistance","Its length","The blood's viscosity"],"Veins are very compliant and hold most of the blood volume.","F"),
(H,"About what fraction of the body's blood is in the veins at rest?","About two-thirds",["About 10%","About one-third","Almost all of it"],"Veins act as a reservoir because of their high capacitance.","F"),
(H,"A vein with low transmural pressure appears:","Elliptical (partly collapsed)",["Perfectly round","Absent","Thick-walled"],"As pressure inside rises, it becomes round.","C"),
(H,"In the legs, venous flow typically falls during:","Inspiration, because abdominal pressure rises as the diaphragm descends",["Expiration","Valsalva release","Distal compression"],"In the arms and IVC near the heart, flow increases with inspiration.","C"),
(H,"The calf muscle pump moves venous blood toward the heart by:","Squeezing deep veins during muscle contraction, with valves preventing backflow",["Pumping arterial blood","Raising venous pressure in the feet","Closing the arteries"],"Valve failure leads to reflux and venous hypertension.","F"),
(H,"In a standing adult, ankle venous pressure is about:","100 mmHg, mostly from hydrostatic pressure",["0 mmHg","10 mmHg","500 mmHg"],"Walking lowers it as the muscle pump empties the veins.","C"),
(H,"Collateral vessels around a chronic arterial occlusion:","Provide alternate pathways with higher resistance than the original artery",["Lower resistance below normal","Carry no flow","Only form in veins"],"Flow beyond the occlusion is damped and may be inadequate during exercise.","F"),
(H,"A 'steal' phenomenon occurs when:","Blood is diverted from one bed to another lower-resistance bed, such as reversed vertebral flow in subclavian steal",["A vein drains into an artery","A valve leaks","Viscosity increases"],"Proximal subclavian stenosis lowers arm pressure, pulling blood backward down the vertebral artery.","F"),
(H,"In subclavian steal, vertebral artery flow on the affected side is:","Reversed or showing systolic deceleration",["Normal and antegrade","Absent in all cases","Pulsatile venous"],"Exercising the arm can increase the reversal.","C"),
(H,"Pulsatile flow in arteries differs from steady flow because:","Inertia and acceleration add extra energy losses and pressure changes",["It has no friction","It never becomes turbulent","It always has lower velocities"],"Pulsatility creates more complex velocity profiles than steady flow.","C"),
(H,"The aortic Windkessel effect means:","The elastic aorta stretches in systole and recoils in diastole, keeping flow continuous",["The aorta stiffens in systole","Blood flows backward in diastole","The aorta pumps blood"],"Stiff aortas in older people raise systolic pressure and pulse pressure.","C"),
(H,"Pulse pressure is:","Systolic pressure minus diastolic pressure",["Systolic plus diastolic","Mean arterial pressure","Heart rate times stroke volume"],"A wide pulse pressure occurs in severe AR and with stiff arteries.","F"),
(H,"Mean arterial pressure is approximately:","Diastolic pressure + one-third of pulse pressure",["Systolic − diastolic","Systolic ÷ 2","Diastolic × 2"],"For 120/80: 80 + (40 ÷ 3) ≈ 93 mmHg.","F"),
(H,"Blood pressure 130/70 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure?","About 90 mmHg",["About 100 mmHg","About 60 mmHg","About 130 mmHg"],"MAP ≈ 70 + (60 ÷ 3) = 90 mmHg.","C"),
(H,"Why can a 70% diameter stenosis reduce flow while a 30% stenosis does not?","Resistance rises sharply with narrowing (radius to the fourth power) and energy losses multiply",["A 30% stenosis has higher resistance","Flow never changes with stenosis","Velocity falls in a stenosis"],"Mild narrowing is compensated by distal vasodilation; severe narrowing exceeds it.","C"),
(H,"Distal arteriolar vasodilation in response to a stenosis:","Lowers downstream resistance to maintain flow at rest",["Raises resistance","Stops all flow","Causes the stenosis"],"This reserve is used up during exercise, causing symptoms such as claudication or angina.","C"),
(H,"Reynolds number depends on:","Velocity, vessel diameter, blood density and viscosity",["Pressure only","Heart rate only","Frequency of the transducer"],"Re = (velocity × diameter × density) ÷ viscosity.","F"),
(H,"A murmur is heard because:","Turbulent flow causes vibrations of tissue",["Laminar flow makes sound","Valves always click","Blood is viscous"],"Turbulence from stenosis, regurgitation or high flow can be heard and felt (a thrill).","F"),
(H,"Increasing heart rate with constant stroke volume makes cardiac output:","Increase",["Decrease","Stay the same","Fall to zero"],"CO = HR × SV. At very fast rates, stroke volume falls because filling time is short.","F"),
(H,"A stenosis causes the greatest pressure drop:","At the narrowest point and just beyond it, where kinetic energy is highest and turbulence causes losses",["Far upstream","In normal segments","At the heart only"],"Some pressure is recovered further downstream as velocity falls.","C"),
(H,"Blood flow is steady (not pulsatile) mainly in:","Capillaries and veins",["The aorta","The pulmonary artery","The carotid artery"],"Pulsatility is damped as blood passes through the resistance vessels.","F"),
(H,"Bernoulli's principle explains why, in a narrowing:","Velocity rises and pressure falls, with total energy conserved (ignoring losses)",["Pressure and velocity both rise","Pressure rises and velocity falls","Flow rate falls"],"Doppler uses this to estimate pressure gradients from velocity.","F"),
(H,"Flow volume through a vessel is 6 cm³/s with a cross-sectional area of 0.5 cm². What is the mean velocity?","12 cm/s",["3 cm/s","6 cm/s","0.08 cm/s"],"Velocity = flow ÷ area = 6 ÷ 0.5 = 12 cm/s.","C"),
(H,"Velocity in a vessel is 20 cm/s with an area of 2 cm². The area narrows to 0.5 cm². What is the velocity in the narrowing?","80 cm/s",["5 cm/s","40 cm/s","20 cm/s"],"Continuity: 20 × 2 = V₂ × 0.5, so V₂ = 80 cm/s.","C"),
(H,"Hematocrit affects blood flow mainly by changing:","Viscosity",["Vessel length","Vessel radius","Heart rate only"],"Higher hematocrit raises viscosity and resistance.","F"),
(H,"Which statement about laminar and disturbed flow is correct?","Disturbed flow (eddies) can occur at bifurcations without true turbulence",["Disturbed flow and turbulence are identical","Laminar flow is chaotic","Bifurcations always have plug flow"],"Flow separation at a carotid bulb is normal and causes brief reversal near the wall.","C"),
(H,"Normal flow separation in the carotid bulb appears on color Doppler as:","A small area of reversed color along the outer wall",["Aliasing throughout","Complete absence of flow","Mosaic turbulence filling the vessel"],"It is a normal finding; its absence can suggest plaque.","C"),
]
